.TH AILX 1 "2026-11-27" "ailx 0.2" "AI Linux"
.SH NAME
ailx \- AI Linux system layer and compute engine: capabilities, models, runtime, tuning, profiles
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ailx
[\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-nvml\fR] \fICOMMAND\fR ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
Commands: \fBstatus\fR, \fBhardware\fR, \fBresources\fR, \fBdoctor\fR, \fBknobs\fR,
\fBprofile\fR list|show|plan|apply, \fBconfirm\fR, \fBrollback\fR [\-\-recover], \fBhistory\fR,
\fBengine\fR list|install|verify|use|remove|env, \fBimage\fR status|stage|confirm|rollback,
\fBsystem\fR plan|install|uninstall|sudoers.
.PP
Compute engine: \fBcaps\fR (hardware capabilities and usable backends),
\fBmodel\fR list|inspect|plan (GGUF/safetensors analysis, placement, memory, bottlenecks),
\fBrecommend\fR, \fBrun\fR start|status|list|logs|stop|rm (managed llama-server instances with
admission control, priorities and optional, experimental CPU placement),
\fBtune\fR plan|calibrate|run|list|show|verify|reset (screening, confirmation and adoption against the
engine defaults; lossy candidates need \fB\-\-allow\-lossy\fR and a quality reference).
Memory predictions are uncalibrated estimates until hardware-validated.
.PP
Profile settings that are not hardware-validated are experimental and are only applied with
\fB\-\-include\-experimental\fR. Every change is journaled, verified and can be rolled back;
privileged writes go through \fI/usr/libexec/ailx/ailx-apply-helper\fR.
.SH FILES
/etc/ailx/ailx.toml, /var/lib/ailx (state, engines, models)
.SH SEE ALSO
ailx-bench(1)
